The callers of ieee80211_ie_split_ric use assoc_data->ie (locally supplied, from user space via nl80211 — actually assoc IEs come from userspace wpa_supplicant, so semi-trusted). skip_ie assumes "validly formed IEs buffer" — callers pass user-controlled IEs that may be malformed. This is a known CVE class (CVE-2022-41674? no; there were fixes to skip_ie). In this snapshot, ieee80211_ie_split_ric at util.c:1607 reads ies[pos+ext] where pos+ext < ielen checked — ok. skip_ie reads ies[pos+1] when pos < ielen but pos+1 could equal ielen → 1-byte OOB read, and pos += 2 + len may overshoot. The callers: mlme.c passes assoc_data->ie from userspace-supplied assoc IE buffer (wpa_supplicant), so untrusted-ish. That matches the branch (element-id consumed then length read with <2 remaining).

But I must finalize now with at most four targeted calls; I've used several already. Let me decide findings:

Finding 1 (primary, medium/high): cfg80211_gen_new_ie loop at scan.c:307 — `while (tmp_old + tmp_old[1] + 2 - ie <= ielen)` reads length byte tmp_old[1] when exactly 1 byte remains (ielen - (tmp_old - ie) == 1), matching the mptcp seed shape: consume ID byte then read length byte without remaining>=2 check. Attacker-controlled via beacon/probe MBSSID profiles (cfg80211_parse_mbssid_data → cfg80211_gen_new_ie with ie/ielen from frame). Note upstream fix added length checks (cfg80211_gen_new_ie OOB, fixed later with `if (tmp_old + tmp_old[1] + 2 - ie > ielen) break;` plus length validation). Actually the upstream fix for this exact loop: commit "wifi: cfg80211: fix possible OOB in cfg80211_gen_new_ie" — hmm, there was CVE-2022-... Actually there is a real known bug: cfg80211_gen_new_ie was fixed in 2023? Let me recall: commit d8a0efd0cf62 ("wifi: cfg80211: fix OOB read in cfg80211_gen_new_ie")? There was indeed a syzbot report on cfg80211_gen_new_ie reading OOB (mbssid). I recall a fix in 2022: "cfg80211: fix kernel panic and leak in cfg80211_gen_new_ie" — hmm. Regardless, the loop condition reads tmp_old[1] when only 1 byte remains; that's a literal 1-byte OOB read of a beacon-adjacent buffer — exactly the seed mechanism shape.

Wait — careful analysis of `while (tmp_old + tmp_old[1] + 2 - ie <= ielen)`: the length byte is read as part of the loop *condition* itself. If exactly 1 byte remains (tmp_old = ie + ielen - 1), then tmp_old[1] is read OOB (1 past end). Yes — the length byte is dereferenced before any remaining>=2 check. Same for the second loop at 367 with sub_copy. sub_copy is a kmemdup'd copy of subie_len bytes — reading sub_copy[1] past its end reads adjacent heap (slab) memory — 1-byte heap OOB read, KASAN-detectable. And profile (the subelement) comes from cfg80211_merge_profile output which is derived from the attacker-controlled MBSSID elements. Actually in cfg80211_gen_new_ie the `subelement` arg is `profile` (merged IE data of length profile_len) — its last byte could be an ID with no length byte → loop condition at 367 reads sub_copy[1] OOB. Also memcpy(pos, tmp_new, tmp_new[1]+2) could over-read beyond sub_copy if a length byte claims more than remaining — but the while condition `tmp_new + tmp_new[1] + 2 - sub_copy <= subie_len` guards memcpy within subie_len... except it reads the length byte itself OOB in the boundary case. Also `memcpy(pos, tmp_new, tmp_new[1] + 2)` writes into new_ie which is IEEE80211_MAX_DATA_LEN; gen_new_ie sums old IEs + subelements; there was a known overflow of new_ie? The loop bounds total copies by ielen+subie_len roughly; new_ie is IEEE80211_MAX_DATA_LEN (2304) and ielen can be larger? cfg80211_parse_mbssid_data is called with ielen from frame length, which can exceed 2304? Beacon IEs can be up to... mgmt frame len limited. Not going to overclaim; focus on the OOB read of the length byte.

